{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>To You silence is praise</b> — David composed this psalm when the Ark was present, or one of the poets composed it at the building of the Temple, and this is the correct view in my eyes." "<b>And silence</b> — from the root of דומה (to be like), or it is as in: 'Only to Elohim my soul is silent' (Psalm 62:2), like the meaning of 'Be silent to YHWH and wait for Him' (Psalm 37:7), 'Remain silent until we reach you' (2 Kings 2:3, דומו עד הגיענו אליכם). And the meaning: for praise waits for You in Zion from those who come there to praise at the place of the Ark, and there a vow is paid to You."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 65, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:32:43.018418529Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}